#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""port_scanner -- a small TCP connect scanner for your own machines.

One of the first things I wrote while teaching myself networking
(May 2026). The original asked for an IP address and tried 11 ports one
at a time, printing the open ones. This is the updated version of the
same idea. What the previous update added:

* Threaded (up to 32 workers) instead of strictly sequential -- a closed
  or firewalled host no longer costs a full second per port, serially.
* 25 well-known ports, each with its service name (the original's 11
  are all still in there).
* Open / closed / filtered reported as three distinct states.

What this update (v1.2) adds:

* Scan profiles: ``--profile quick`` is the original 25 ports;
  ``standard`` is a curated Top-100; ``deep`` is a Top-1000. A bigger
  service-name table (~285 well-known assignments) names what the
  wider profiles find.
* Better accuracy: a port that stays silent (a "filtered" verdict) is
  retried once at a longer timeout (2 seconds) before the label is
  final -- one 1-second pass mislabels slow or firewalled services.
  Every line now shows the connect latency in milliseconds, so a fast
  refusal reads differently from a slow one.
* Open ports on the risky list (SMB, RDP, Docker's unauthenticated
  API, Redis, ...) are called out with a plain-English reason.
* Banner probing covers the usual web-alt ports (80, 8000, 8080,
  8888, 9000). TLS ports are never sent a plaintext probe -- only a
  banner the service volunteers is read there.

Usage:
    python3 port_scanner [target] [--banner] [--timeout 1.0]
                         [--profile quick|standard|deep]

    target    an IP address or hostname (you'll be asked if omitted)
    --banner  also grab service banners on open ports
    --timeout per-port timeout in seconds (default: 1.0)
    --profile which port set to scan (default: quick, the original 25)

Standard library only -- no dependencies.

AUTHORIZED USE: only scan systems you own or have explicit permission
to test. Scanning other people's systems without permission may be
illegal and is not what this tool is for. Start with 127.0.0.1 -- your
own machine is the intended target.

This script grew up into a full desktop app -- see the QuantumCat SOC
Toolkit at https://quantumcatsoc.gumroad.com/l/quantumcat-soc-toolkit
"""

